  <title id="bdl03npesupervised-title">Framing Bayesian inference as a supervised learning problem</title>
  <desc id="bdl03npesupervised-desc">Left, top: a scatter of 320 simulated pairs with theta from 0 to 90 on the horizontal axis and x from 0 to 25 on the vertical axis; x rises steeply to a maximum of about 16 near theta equal to 25 and then falls slowly, with a spread that grows with theta. A horizontal line marks the observation x o equal to 13. Left, bottom: the posterior for x o equal to 13, the horizontal cut through the joint distribution, with a narrow peak near theta equal to 10 and a wider peak near theta equal to 50. Right: three observations, x o equal to 13, 15 and 16, enter a single trained neural network, which returns a posterior for each: two separated peaks for 13, two closer peaks for 15 and one broad peak near theta equal to 25 for 16.</desc>
